Q6: never join, host or offer a Wi-Fi network

Upstream can move a transfer to another Wi-Fi network: hdl/hotspot.rs
runs `nmcli` (and `iw`) to join the Wi-Fi Direct group or hotspot whose
SSID and password the peer sends (join_wifi), or to host a hotspot of its
own for the peer to join (start_hotspot). Both take the machine off its
network, both run external programs, and the network joined is the
peer's choice (spec §5, Q6). The sender also invites this: its
connection request claims it can host a Wi-Fi Direct group and a hotspot
and sends our LAN address (MediumMetadata), and it presents itself as
Windows, because the phone only asks a Windows peer for that role switch.

Sukkula never changes the network and never runs a program (spec S8,
non-goals in §1). With the bandwidth upgrade gone (previous patch)
nothing calls hotspot.rs any more; this deletes it, drops tokio's
"process" feature so that nothing in the library can spawn a process,
and makes the connection request and response say what is true: medium
WIFI_LAN only, no role or address metadata, OS type Linux (as the
receive path already said). The PACKET_SEND_META environment knob that
switched those experiments goes too; a library does not read the
environment.

Findings: Q6 (spec §5), S8. Report: docs/UPSTREAM-QUICKSHARE.md Q6, R14.
